Honduras is one of the poorest countries  in Central and South America it has the  highest murder rate in the world almost  double the rate of the next closest  contender  it's a place ravaged by the drug trade  and plagued by political instability a  place where the military recently ousted  the president after he began exploring  his options to rewrite the Constitution  and extend his own term it may seem like  one of the last places on earth where  businesses want to invest and outsiders  would want to move but there's a new  idea about to be tried in Honduras it's  called a Zed a politically autonomous  privately run zone that supporters  believe could transform not only  Honduras but the entire developing world  the laws are all in place investors are  lined up and ready to go  and the big questions about to be  answered will this actually work  one of the biggest challenges of poor  person faces in the world today  is the inability to access good law and  governance Zach Casares is the executive  director of the startup cities Institute  a research organization based out of  Guatemala's University of Francisco  marroquín on the one hand we are really  good at making better physical  technologies cell phones always get  better computers always get better  cameras always get better on the other  hand you have social technologies you  have the ways in which we organize  ourselves our law and governance systems  these are not subject to entrepreneurial  trial and error in fact they're  essentially locked away from competition  and innovation when you see someone who  is in grinding poverty but they have a  smartphone you can really see this  paradox on display and what I'm  suggesting is let's bring the same kind  of progress the same kind of benefit of  entrepreneurial trial-and-error to  social tech that we already know works  in physical tech because fundamentally  both of these things are technology and  they are open to innovation the way that  it works is that it designates  particular zones that enjoy a high  degree of autonomy in particular in  areas of commercial law independent  police force labor law various other  kinds of things the idea is to develop  different areas in Honduras with  institutions that the Reformers hope  will be better for the economic  development of the countries the  Honduran Congress first passed  legislation creating special zones in  2011 but the project hit a roadblock  when one of its most high-profile  proponents economist Paul Romer pulled  out and disavowed the project citing  transparency concerns the Supreme Court  soon thereafter declared the plan  unconstitutional the Congress then  amended the Constitution and passed a  new version of the legislation this  opened the door for what in Honduras are  known as the zones for employment and  economic development or zetz it's a very  exciting moment because this project if  it accomplishes what it's capable of  doing will demonstrate inside of  Honduras and to the world that the  capacity for solving problems and for  creating jobs  particular can go forward with a  velocity that very few people have been  expecting mark Klugman is a former  Reagan speechwriter and board member on  the committee for the adoption of best  practices the board that has final  approval over proposed zones Klugman has  worked with reformers in several  countries over the last few decades and  until now he's always approached reform  in a more orthodox way by tackling one  issue at a time I had an awakening which  was what will never arrive this way  Klugman likes to refer to the idea as a  leap zone visualized as a chair with  four legs legal economic administrative  and political take out one leg and the  chair loses stability and investors flee  what happens if you say our constant  will be time we want all of them  immediately and simultaneously the model  is the constant that's the starting  point is everything and then the  variable becomes what space land area  right now you have people who live in  places that that have bad governance in  the case of Honduras they have very  serious governance challenges and you  have people who are willing to risk  their lives on extremely dangerous  journeys across national borders to  access other forms of law and governance  they're voting with their feet for  different systems  I think startup cities is a much more  humane way of dealing with ISM so for me  what startup cities is about is actually  lowering the barriers of entry to good  governance for the average person  no Hondurans EDS have yet been launched  but Klugman expects the committee to  begin reviewing proposals in the next  several months in the meantime there are  some existing areas in Honduras that  might act as a preview of what's to come  you  you
 

